WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.680
Technically strong response work can still fail operationally if communication is poor.

00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:09.700
Incidents affect systems, people, business services, legal obligations,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.000
privacy expectations, customer trust, and executive decision-making.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.620
A playbook should define communication and coordination rules before the team is under

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:19.640
pressure.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:25.300
Internal coordination often includes the SOC, IT operations, endpoint teams,

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:32.320
identity teams, cloud teams, network teams, application owners, legal, privacy, HR,

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:35.580
communications, business owners, and executives.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:37.640
Not every incident needs every group,

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.260
but the playbook should explain when to involve them.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:45.120
A suspicious login for a single user may start with the SOC and identity team.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:50.540
A possible data exposure may require privacy and legal review. A ransomware event may

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:52.600
require IT operations,

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.260
business continuity, communications, executives, and possibly external partners.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.800
External coordination must be handled carefully. Vendors may provide logs,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.060
product support, or managed service assistance.

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:11.400
Customers may need updates if their service is affected. Insurers may have notification

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.740
requirements.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.520
Regulators may have reporting timelines. Law enforcement or government partners may be

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.060
appropriate in some circumstances.

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.760
The playbook should not make every responder responsible for external statements. It

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:29.700
should identify who is authorized to coordinate outside the organization and when

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.520
escalation is required.

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:37.180
Good playbook communication rules answer four questions: who updates whom, when,

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.360
through which channel, and with what level of detail.

00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:45.700
A technical channel may be appropriate for responder coordination. A ticket or case record

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:47.920
may be the official system of record.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:52.780
Executive updates should be concise and focused on impact, current status,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:55.560
decisions needed, and next update time.

00:01:55.780 --> 00:02:00.980
Legal or privacy discussions may require restricted channels. Sensitive evidence should

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.880
not be posted broadly.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.580
Responders should avoid speculation. It is acceptable to say that something is under

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:08.920
investigation,

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.780
suspected, confirmed, contained, recovered, or unresolved.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.240
It is risky to guess about root cause, impact, attribution,

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.580
or data exposure before evidence supports the statement.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.560
Stakeholders need clarity,

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.500
not confident uncertainty. A playbook can help by providing status language and update

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.120
templates that separate facts from assumptions.

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.220
Escalation is not a sign of failure. It is how the organization brings the right authority

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.800
to the right decision.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:43.300
Some containment actions may affect production services. Some notifications may have legal

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:44.640
consequences.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:49.500
Some recovery choices may require business risk acceptance. A playbook should identify

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.320
severity thresholds,

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.620
escalation contacts, backup contacts, and decision owners.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:02:57.760
When communication is planned,

00:02:57.980 --> 00:03:02.460
responders can focus on the incident instead of inventing coordination paths in the middle

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:03.400
of it.
